1. COUNTING AND MATCHING AGREE ON FINITE SETS
   C = ['bishop', 'knight', 'pawn', 'rook']
   D = ['east', 'north', 'south', 'west']
   |C| = 4, |D| = 4
     bishop  <-> east
     knight  <-> north
     pawn    <-> south
     rook    <-> west
   one-to-one, nothing left over:  True
   can C match ['east', 'north', 'south']?  every pairing leaves one of C over,
   because |C| = 4 and |E| = 3.

2. THE BARS MEAN SIZE, AND SIZE DEPENDS ON THE KIND OF THING
   |-4|          = 4           absolute value of a number
   |{-4}|        = 1           cardinality of a one-member set
   |{-4, 4}|     = 2           cardinality of a two-member set
   |{1, 1, 1}|   = 1           a set does not count repeats

3. THE PRODUCT RULE  |A x B| = |A| . |B|
   |A| = 4, |B| = 7, |A x B| = 28 = 4 x 7
   Same rule, read as types: a pair type has as many values as the
   product of its parts. Some sizes every programmer already knows:
     bool                        2
     uint8                     256
     uint16                 65,536
     (bool, uint8)             512   product type: 2 x 256
     (uint8, uint8)         65,536   product type: 256 x 256 = uint16
     bool | uint8              258   sum type (tagged union): 2 + 256
     Optional[uint8]           257   None | uint8: 1 + 256
     uint8 -> bool       1.158e+77   function type: 2^256 tables

4. CARDINALITY OF A COLUMN
   a table of 12 rows:   name   country   role
     COUNT(DISTINCT name)     = 12   = |{ann, bob, cy, dee, eli, fay, ...}|
     COUNT(DISTINCT country)  =  4   = |{DE, FR, PL, US}|
     COUNT(DISTINCT role)     =  2   = |{admin, user}|
   A query planner keeps these numbers. An index on `name` narrows
   a lookup to 1 row; an index on `role` narrows it to 10. The
   cardinality of the column is what decides whether the index is
   worth reading.

5. CARDINALITY OF A RELATIONSHIP: HOW MANY ROWS ON EACH SIDE
   KNA1 (customers)  = C001, C002, C003, C004
   VBRK (invoices)   = INV1->C001, INV2->C001, INV3->C002, INV4->C001, INV5->C004
   customers per invoice:  [1]   every invoice names exactly one customer
   invoices per customer:  [0, 1, 3]   a customer has 0, 1 or many
   SE11 notation, check table first:        1:CN
   DDL notation, foreign key table first:   [0..*,1]
   Same relationship, the two sides written in opposite orders. The
   numbers are cardinalities of sets: |{customers of INV1}| = 1 and
   |{invoices of C001}| = 3, and the notation records the range.

6. MATCHING WORKS WHERE COUNTING STOPS: N AND THE EVEN NUMBERS
   Pair n with 2n. The first few pairs, and the rule for all of them:
     0<->0  1<->2  2<->4  3<->6  4<->8  5<->10  6<->12  7<->14  8<->16  9<->18  ...   n <-> 2n
   in the first 100,000 pairs, every even number below 200,000 is hit
   exactly once:  True
   The rule never runs out on either side, so by the matching
   definition |N| = |even numbers|, though one is a proper part of
   the other. Counting has no opinion here; it never finishes.

7. AND MATCHING CAN FAIL: THE DIAGONAL
   Suppose someone claims to have listed EVERY infinite 0/1 sequence,
   row 0, row 1, row 2, ... Here are 8 rows of such a list:
     row 0:  [0]  1   0   1   0   1   0   1
     row 1:   1  [1]  1   1   1   1   1   1
     row 2:   0   1  [0]  1   0   1   0   1
     row 3:   1   1   0  [0]  1   1   0   0
     row 4:   0   1   0   1  [0]  1   0   1
     row 5:   1   1   1   1   1  [1]  1   1
     row 6:   0   1   1   0   1   0  [0]  1
     row 7:   1   1   1   1   1   1   1  [1]
     diagonal:         0   1   0   0   0   1   0   1
     flipped:          1   0   1   1   1   0   1   0
   the flipped diagonal differs from row r in column r, for every r:
   [True, True, True, True, True, True, True, True]
   So it is a 0/1 sequence that is on no row of the list. Whatever
   list is offered, this builds a sequence it missed. No matching of
   N onto the set of 0/1 sequences exists: that set is UNCOUNTABLE.

8. WHY A PROGRAMMER SHOULD CARE: MORE FUNCTIONS THAN PROGRAMS
   A program is a finite string over a finite alphabet. Those can be
   listed, shortest first, alphabetically within a length:
     a  b  aa  ab  ba  bb  aaa  aab  aba  abb  baa  bab  bba  bbb  ...
   So |programs| = |N|. A function from N to {0, 1} is an infinite
   0/1 sequence, and section 7 showed those cannot be listed. There
   are more such functions than there are programs, so some of them
   have no program at all. That is the cardinality argument for the
   existence of uncomputable functions, and it needs no example.
